<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishme-Dagan I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> I was the elder son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>, the Amorite ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>, in the 18th century BCE. His father installed him as king at Ekallatum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasmah-addu">Yasmah-Addu</a> ruled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. After Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>'s death the realm broke apart, and Ishme-Dagan held only Ekallatum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> competed for the region.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>'s father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>, was an Amorite king, originally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, who formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> around 1808 BCE and ruled from Shubat-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>. He placed his oldest son on the throne of Ekallatum and his youngest on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. Ishme-Dagan ruled the south-eastern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, and his realm of influence included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. Excavated letters show tensions in the family: Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> calls Ishme-Dagan a forceful soldier not afraid to risk his own skin, while his letters to the younger son are less generous, and Ishme-Dagan seems to have taken up that censure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His main challenge was to keep his enemies in check. To his south lay King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadusha">Dadusha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, who became his chief enemy; a year-name coined by Dadusha commemorates a victory over an army led by Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>. With his father he campaigned against Qabra and Nurugum, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. According to letters of the period, once he had conquered it he allowed some prisoners to join his army and gave special treatment to skilled ones. His troops also exterminated the nomadic tribe of the Ya'ilanum.</p><p>The Assyrian King List, which is much later and somewhat apocryphal, credits him with a reign of 40 years, but a list of eponyms unearthed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kanesh</a> in 2003 shows that it lasted around 11 years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After his father's death Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> could not hold the realm. He lost most of his domain and was reduced to holding Ashur and Ekallatum, despite counter-offensives to regain the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/year-name">Year-names</a> of the eighth and ninth years of King Ibalpiel show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> destroyed the armies of Ashur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. Afterwards he kept tolerable relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, Eshnunna and Mari. Hammurabi at least once requested reinforcements, and Ishme-Dagan responded, though grudgingly. A letter once thought to be his to his brother was shown to be from Ishme-Addu of Ashnakku, which disproved chronologies based on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ishme-Dagan I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shamshi-Adad ruled as 'king of the universe', placing his elder son <b>Ishme-Dagan</b> at Ekallatum and his younger son Yasmah-Addu at Mari.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a></li></ul></section>
